   "outcome": "Two jobs reached payment and both ledgers add up. Jamie's drain job: $185 captured, $18.50 fee, $141.52 to the pro and $24.98 to the helper in one batch, and $37 of the hold never charged. Dana's water heater: two milestone captures totalling $1,450, $104.40 of retainage held, and $290 of buffer released. The person-only link worked: Dana raised her limit through it. Luis, a real pro, was hired for the first time at $110, but stalled when the seeded customer never answered his change order. No money went to the wrong place. But a person's release on a milestone that needed inspection paid at once, skipping the inspector. A bare release call also paid a milestone whose checks had failed. A demo pro stamped photos before the job was posted and sent faucet evidence for a water heater, and the red team's onboarding code was used up before the pro could use it.",
   "fixes": [
    "A release never skips a required inspection unless the person explicitly waives it. Releasing over failed checks needs each failed check acknowledged, and request_fix needs a reason.",
    "Photos must be taken after work started, and AFTER photos after the BEFORE photos. Demo pros stamp their photos in order and send scenes that fit the job (water heaters included), with fresh photos on each fix round.",
    "Demo customers answer change orders within a couple of minutes. A pro can withdraw a pending change order, and a refused submission stores nothing.",
    "Pros can always get a fresh sign-in link through readiness. Withdrawing an offer and re-offering keeps the negotiation history, and the price can't go up.",
    "In demo mode, demo pros come within minutes. With no saved payment method, accepting an offer returns an approval link rather than charging the test card. Agents can apply scope suggestions.",
    "Website: in-page confirmations instead of browser pop-ups, a single clear line on the release screen, a home page that works for people without an agent, and photo times shown relative to the job.",
    "Permit numbers in Austin's formats are recognised."
   ]
  },
